# Vision Code Lexicon

This document defines a strict, non-overlapping meaning for selected terms used across `vision/`.
Only words with an unambiguous single definition are included here.

## How to judge a word (mandatory)

When asked whether a word fits this lexicon or “has a definition,” judge **meaning in `vision/` code**, not whether the token appears in lexicon.

**Lexicon headwords name one concept as a single word** (or an established pattern token such as `` `at_uv` `` / `` `at_xy` ``, `` `_by_` ``, `` `_from_` ``, `` `_over_` ``, or `` `a_*` ``). Established task compounds that are one concept in prose (`moving_bar`, `spot`) may appear as compounds. Glue-compound headwords that join two independent words without forming one concept are forbidden. Exception: ``val_from`` (see ``### val_from``). Forbidden: hyphen spellings inside lexicon text (`val-from`).

**Banned words never get a headword.** A token that is only a forbidden substitute (listed under another concept’s **Forbidden**) must **not** have its own ``### \`word\`` entry — not even an entry whose body says “Forbidden / do not use.” Ban it only by naming it under the canonical concept’s Forbidden line. Creating ``### \`name\`` / ``### \`tag\`` / ``### \`gain\`` / ``### \`polarity\`` (etc.) solely to forbid them is itself a lexicon violation.

**Banned-substitute map (lexicon-wide — no `tag` headword; `tag` is forbidden wherever it has been a substitute):** The token ``tag`` / ``_tag`` / ``*_tag`` / ``*tag*`` is banned project-wide — it is a substitute that **never gets its own headword** (per the rule above). It must be replaced with the canonical concept the role actually fills:
- Human-facing display string, figure/report series name, overlay key, snapshot stage, axis label, or display dedup-suffix → ``label`` (see ``### label``).
- CLI / opts parse atom, filename stem fragment, or parse-input kwarg → ``token`` (see ``### token``).
- CLI / opts sub-option under a named parent → ``key`` (see ``### key``).
- String token from a fixed enum (``syn_mode``, ``z_mode``, ``vote_mode``, param ``mode``) or its collection bag → ``mode`` (see ``### mode``).
- Moving-bar condition id string ``left_bright_w1`` or its bag → ``spec`` / ``spec_token`` (see ``### spec``).

This rule is enforced by adding the corresponding ``tag`` ban to each canonical headword’s **Forbidden** line; legacy in-code ``*tag`` / ``*_tag`` identifiers are forbidden to keep (rename, don’t alias).

**Evidence:** List every function, method, class, attribute, constant, CLI/opts key, and variable/parameter whose name contains the word (compounds included: `foo_word`, `word_bar`, `WordBaz`). If meanings differ, partition the list into one class per distinct definition; put unrelated homographs (library APIs, other biophysics, …) in an “other” class — still list them.

Then answer only:

1. **Standalone definition?** Can this concept (per class under review) be written as a `### \`word\`` entry (Definition / Example / Forbidden)? Yes = one coherent meaning; no = generic pattern / fragment / prose glue / banned substitute. Multiple classes → say how many standalones.
2. **Collision?** Does any class duplicate another lexicon concept (or another in-code concept that needs its own entry)? Same role under two names → collision (name both).
3. **Propose rename:** For **every** inventoried name (all classes, including “other” / keep-as-is), propose the canonical identifier it should have under current lexicon rules. List them all in one table — do not omit names that stay unchanged.

**Answer shape:** (1) name inventory by meaning class; (2) standalone yes/no + one-line meaning per class; (3) collision yes/no + other term(s); (4) full rename table with columns `current` | `proposed` | `reason` (reason cites the lexicon headword or “keep”). Do not answer with only “it appears / doesn’t appear in the lexicon.”

## Nouns (A-Z)

### `a` / `a_*`

Definition: Multiplicative network / schema parameter — a tunable (or fixed) factor applied inside the model, always named with prefix ``a_``. Covers forward gains (`a_in`, `a_out`, `a_h`, `a_ca`, `a_sti_radius`, `a_syn_exc`, `a_syn_inh`) and cost affine `a_gt`. Bounds bag `A_LO` / `A_HI`.
Example: `params["a_out"]`, `v_out = relu(v − v_th) · a_out`, `ScatterConn(..., a_syn_exc=A_SYN_EXC, a_syn_inh=A_SYN_INH)`, `gt_aff = a_gt * gt + bias_gt`
Forbidden: `gain` (including `GAIN_*`, prose “gain param”); do not name these with `scale` or `weight`. Do not use bare `a` as a local for other quantities.

### `alias` / `aliases`

Definition: Registered CLI / opts group shorthand that expands to a fixed set of concrete ``task`` tokens (or related part keys) — singular ``alias`` is one such token (``spot``, ``moving_bar``); plural ``aliases`` is the bag or a collection of those tokens. Allowed only in the established bags ``TASK_ALIASES``, ``PART_COST_SCALE_ALIASES``, and ``I_CLI_*_TASKS`` (and resolvers / expanders that consume them).
Example: `TASK_ALIASES`, `resolve_i_sti_alias("spot_bright")` → ``"spot"``, `expand_tasks`, `--i-sti spot=…`
Forbidden: `paradigm` (including ``I_STI_PARADIGMS``, ``resolve_i_sti_paradigm``, prose “sti paradigm” / “per-paradigm”). Do not coin a second surface spelling for the same concrete task outside those bags (see coding-rules alias ban).

### `adam` / `adams`

Definition: First-/second-order optimizer moments for the trainable vector ``z`` — the ``exp_avg`` / ``exp_avg_sq`` tensors and the per-param bags expanded from or packed into them. Singular ``adam`` is one such bag or the concept; plural ``adams`` is a collection of those bags (or the pair of m/v arrays). Compounds name the role (``adams_m`` / ``adams_v``, ``z_adams``, ``run_adams``, ``adam_init``, ``adam_iter``, ``save_adam``, ``best_adam.npz``). Disk/npz field for the step count remains ``iter``; the in-code local pulled from adam is ``adam_iter``.
Example: `adams_m, adams_v = adams_from_z(exp_avg, exp_avg_sq, schema)`, `z_adams_from_node_vals(...)`, `adams_from_optimizer(...)`, `_load_adams(...)`, `run_adams`, `adam_iter`, `save_adam(...)`, `load_best_adam(...)`
Forbidden: `moment` / `moments` as naming tokens (`moments_from_z`, `moments_m`, `z_moments_*`, `_load_moments`, prose “moment dict”). Do not use `opt` / `opts` for these tensors (`opt` / `opts` are input options only). Do not name the update loop with `adam` — that is ``optimization`` / ``optimize``. Do not use project identifiers ``state`` / ``state_dict`` / ``pstate`` for these bags — that is ``adam`` / ``adams``; library ``torch.optim.Adam``, ``optimizer.state_dict()`` / ``load_state_dict`` / ``optimizer.state``, and library keys ``exp_avg`` / ``exp_avg_sq`` / ``step`` / ``'state'`` may remain at the call site only.

### `all`

Definition: Universal-quantifier / leftover token — not a collection prefix. Allowed only as: (1) train-mode leftover key ``'all'`` (nodes not claimed by an explicit mode); (2) CLI universal token such as ``all_param`` (one mode token applied to every param name); (3) boolean predicates whose head is not a collection noun (``all_active``, ``all_columned``).
Example: `param_modes["bias_gt"] = {"fixed": "all"}`, `all_param`, `all_mode`, `all_init`, `all_idx`
Forbidden: as a prefix on collection / bag names — never `all_hexes`, `all_nodes`, `all_params`, `all_gt`, `all_v`, `all_traces`, …. Use the plural noun alone (`hexes`, `nodes`, `gts`, `vals`, `traces`), or `run_params` / `run_adams` for per-run bags. Do not write `all_` + plural (`all_stis`) or `all_` + singular collection (`all_sti`).

### `b` / `bs`

Definition: Sti-sample index along the parallel-sti axis — a position 0..`n_b`-1 into tensors such as `i_sti` / `trace` shaped `(n_b, n_t, N)`, or which sti slot a cost `entry` belongs to. Singular ``b`` is that index. Plural ``bs`` is a bag/collection of those idxs (e.g. `entry_bs`, `active_bs`). Cardinality ``n_b``. Compounds: `entry_bs`, `active_bs`, `sti_bs`, `sti_b`, `b_sums`, `b_set`, `b_offsets`, `n_t_by_b`, `spot_sti_bs`, `SpotB` (one spot sti configuration for one b).
Example: `for b in range(n_b): ...`, `active_bs = pack.entry_bs.unique(sorted=True)`, `trace[b, t, node]`, `sti_b` when indexing `i_sti` slots separately from readout `b`
Forbidden: `batch`, `batches`, `batch_idx`, `n_batch`, `batched`, and other `*batch*` naming tokens for this axis. Do not suffix the index with `_idx` — use bare `b` (not `b_idx` / `batch_idx`). Library / non-axis uses may remain (e.g. RGBA channel `b`, plotly margin key `b`).

### `bound` / `bounds`

Definition: A geometric limit or extent in space (degrees / hex / clip geometry) — not a train numeric range. Singular ``bound`` is one geometric threshold (e.g. a half-plane clip edge). Plural ``bounds`` is a geometric extent bag or the four-edge sti coverage box. Compounds: ``view_bounds``, ``view_bounds_from_vertices``.
Example: `view_bounds(hexes)`, `view_bounds_from_vertices(x_deg, y_deg)`, `_clip_halfplane(..., bound=)`, loop local `bound` for one clip edge
Forbidden: train / optimize / schema numeric ranges that a ``param`` or ``z`` must stay inside — that is ``clamp`` / ``clamps`` (``z_clamps``, ``schema_clamps``, ``param_clamps``, ``v_clamp``, …). Do not name schema ``lo``/``hi`` bags or optimize clamp tensors with ``bound``.

### `cell` / `cells`

Definition: A neuron type from the connectome (e.g. `"L1"`, `"T4a"`), grouping multiple nodes that share the same biological identity.
Example: `connectome.cells` (connectome vocabulary mapping nodes to cell types)
Forbidden: do not use `node` to refer to a cell type. `cell` is a connectome type; `node` is a specific numbered neuron in the network. Do not use `name` / `names` / `cell_name` / `*_by_name` / `idx_from_name` for this — use `cell` / `cells` / `cell_idx` / `*_by_cell`. Do not use ``label`` for a connectome type — that is ``cell`` (see ``### label``). Forbidden: ``slot`` / ``slots`` as a naming token for param-axis CLI ids or any idx role. Forbidden: ``member`` / ``members`` for cells in a family — use ``cell`` / ``cells``.

### `center` / `centers`

Definition: Spot / RF / cost readout locus at hex-lattice radius 0 — the sti anchor hex (`du=dv=0`), the `entry_radii==0` slot, the RF axis index 0, or the plot row / time trace for that radius. Compounds: `spot_centers` (simultaneous sti anchor hexes), `center_entry_mask`, `center_only`, `center_hex` (sole cost hex when `cost_radius==0`), `pack_center_mask`, `build_spot_center_readout`, `RF_CENTER_RADIUS`, `gt_center`, `v_readout_center`.
Example: `center_entry_mask = (du == 0) & (dv == 0)`, `spot.centers`, `center_only=True`, `RF_CENTER_RADIUS = 0`
Forbidden: do not use `center` for bar-trail midpoint, histogram bin midpoint, node `(u,v)` lookup, or hex-patch vertex bounds — use `mid`, ``bound`` / ``bounds``, or non-`center` hex coords. Do not use `r0` / `R0` as naming tokens (see coding-rules). Library matplotlib layout kwargs (`xanchor='center'`, `ha='center'`) may remain.

### `clamp` / `clamps`

Definition: The numeric range a train-time number must stay inside — schema / ``z`` optimize limits and related magnitude caps. Singular ``clamp`` is one such range (or the concept); plural ``clamps`` is a bag or ``(n_z, 2)`` tensor of those ranges. Edges of a param clamp are schema / ``param_key`` ``lo`` / ``hi`` (bags ``los`` / ``his``). Compounds: ``z_clamps``, ``schema_clamps``, ``_z_clamps``, ``param_clamps`` (CLI/opts stamps for ``lo``/``hi``), ``v_clamp`` (voltage magnitude cap in ``NEURON_CONST``). Init jitter stamps are ``param_jits`` (not ``clamp``).
Example: `z_clamps = schema_clamps(schema, ...)`, `z.clamp_(z_clamps[:, 0], z_clamps[:, 1])`, `param_clamps`, `param_jits`, `session.v_clamp`, `NEURON_CONST['v_clamp']`
Forbidden: `bound` / `bounds` for this role (`z_bounds`, `schema_bounds`, `param_bound`, `param_bounds`, …) — ``bound`` is geometry only (see ``### bound``). Do not name ``jit`` / init jitter as ``clamp`` (use ``param_jits`` / ``jit``). Do not use ``clamp`` for sti ``view`` geometric extent — that is ``bound`` / ``view_bounds``. Library ``torch.clamp`` / ``.clamp_`` / ``.clamp`` (the verb API that enforces a clamp) may remain at call sites.

### `col` / `cols`

Definition: A horizontal subplot-grid index in figure code only — which panel slot within one plot `row` (matplotlib `GridSpec` column, trace column within a component figure).
Example: `n_col`, `col`, `N_COL_GT`, `fig.add_subplot(gs[row, col])`, `layout.n_col`
Forbidden: ``col_idx`` — the integer is bare ``col``. Connectome `column` / `column_id`; CSV table field; hex position; cost `entry` mask — do not use `col` outside plot code. Library matplotlib kwargs like `sharex="col"` may remain.

### `column` / `columns`

Definition: A connectome optic-lobe position identifier — the biological column a neuron is assigned to (`column_id` on disk / per source row; in-memory bag `connectome.column_ids`), mapped to hex `(u, v)` via column-assignment tables.
Example: `connectome.column_ids`, `majority_column_id`, `columns.csv.gz`, `partner_column_id`, `u_by_column_id`, JSON/CSV field `"column_id"`
Forbidden: do not use `column` for a CSV output-table field — use `field`. Do not use `column` for a hex `(u, v)` position — use `hex`. Do not use `col` as a naming token for connectome columns — spell `column` / `column_id` (disk) / `column_ids` (in-memory bag). Do not use `field` for sti view geometry or ``--param`` keys — use `view` / `key`.

### `connectome` / `connectomes`

Definition: The loaded biological neural-circuit dataset (FAFB), instantiated as a `Network` object; the source of `cell`, `syn`, `hex` vocabulary and connectivity structure.
Example: `connectome = session.connectome` (the connectome object used throughout train)
Forbidden: do not use `network` to refer to the connectome object or its biological contents (`cell`, `syn`, `hex`). `network` names the simulation graph, not the biological dataset. Do not abbreviate the connectome object as `C`.

### `contrast` / `contrasts`

Definition: Bright vs dark sti variant (``"bright"`` | ``"dark"``) for spot / moving-bar tasks — which light polarity the sti uses, not a ±1 multiplier.
- ``contrast`` (singular): one token ``"bright"`` or ``"dark"`` (param, pack info field, MovingBarSpec field, dict key).
- ``contrasts`` (plural): a list/set/tuple of those tokens.
The ±1 for a contrast is ``contrast_sign`` (see ``sign``), not ``contrast`` itself.
Example: `contrast_sign(contrast)`, `SPOT_CONTRASTS`, `MOVING_BAR_CONTRASTS`, `GRUNTMAN_CONTRASTS`, `MovingBarSpec.contrast`, `contrast_for_task`, `contrasts=(contrast,)`, pack info ``"contrast"``, figure keys `{contrast: readout}`
Forbidden: `polarity` (including `polarity_sign` and prose “polarity”). Do not use `contrast` for a ±1 — that is `sign` / `contrast_sign`. Do not use `contrast` for intensity / adapt gates or other non-bright/dark senses.

### `cost` / `costs`

Definition: The scalar optimization objective value computed from readouts versus ground truth (loss).
Example: `best_cost = float(final_costs[int(np.argmin(final_costs))])`

### `data`

Definition: The set of parameter output files (`.npy`, `.npz`, `train_opts.json`, etc.) saved under the `data/` subfolder of a run directory.
Example: `os.makedirs(run_data_dir(outdir), exist_ok=True)`
Forbidden: `artifact`, `output` — use `data` instead. Do not use `data` for in-memory forward or plot worksets — use `readout`.

### `const` / `consts`

Definition: Session-injected model scalars that are **not** packed into ``z`` — bag ``NEURON_CONST`` (`cap`, `g_leak`, `e_exc`, `euler`, `delta_ms`, …). Distinct from trainable ``NEURON_SCHEMA['params']``.
Example: `from const_default import NEURON_CONST`, `session.g_leak`, `session.v_clamp`, `NEURON_CONST['cap']`
Forbidden: ``NEURON_PARAM``; naming these with ``param`` / ``optimizable``. Do not name the voltage clamp const ``state_clamp`` — that is ``v_clamp`` (see ``### clamp``).

### `default`

Definition: Numeric/config literal source — scope split by tree.
- **`vision/simulation/**`** (numbered core, figure, analyze, run): allowed only as the module/file name ``const_default`` — the sole numeric literal bag for train, figure, analyze, and run layers. Simulation code defines no ``default_*`` / ``DEFAULT_*`` constants; literals live in ``const_default`` and are imported by role name (``BAR_RADIUS``, ``SYN_SIGN_BINS``, …) or via ``resolve_*`` at CLI/session assembly.
- **`vision/connectome/**`**: connectome-build literals may use noun ``DEFAULT_*`` constants (``DEFAULT_NETWORK_RUN``, ``DEFAULT_RADIUS``, ``DEFAULT_SIDE``, …) for CLI defaults and reference geometry. Not the simulation ``const_default`` module.
Example: `from const_default import RUN_PATH`, `const_default.NEURON_SCHEMA['params']`; connectome `path.DEFAULT_NETWORK_RUN`, `build_hex.DEFAULT_RADIUS`; figure CLI `default=DEFAULT_NETWORK_RUN` importing from connectome ``path``.
Forbidden: In **vision/simulation/** only — ``default`` / ``defaults`` and any project identifier containing ``default`` (``default_*``, ``*_default``, ``DEFAULT_*``, ``_default_*``) except the module name ``const_default``. Do not coin verb/compound substitutes for connectome ``DEFAULT_*`` in simulation (``FALLBACK_*``, ``BUILD_*``, ``BASE_*``, ``ASSIGN_*``, …). Library/API spellings may remain unchanged (``argparse`` keyword ``default=``, dataclass ``default_factory``, ``dict.setdefault``, help ``%(default)s``).

### `edge` / `edges`

Definition: A directed connection between two nodes in the network, carrying a signed `weight` (`edge_weights`) derived from connectome synapse counts (`syn_sign * n_syn` or `syn_sign`).
Example: `source_idxs = np.empty(len(edges), dtype=np.int64)`, `edge_weights`
Forbidden: do not use `syn` to refer to a network edge or its idx. `edge` is a network connection; `syn` is a connectome biological synapse.

### `entry` / `entries`

Definition: One cost comparison unit in a `Pack`: a (cell × radius) or (cell × PD/ND) unit — also the integer slot 0..`n_cost`-1 into pack entry bags. Pack aligns bags `entry_nodes`, `gts`, `cost_scales`, `entry_bs`, `entry_radii` (one slot per entry; `entry_radii` is spot hex-lattice radius). The cost MSE is a scaled sum over entries with ``entry_mask`` True.
Example: `entries = _active_entries(work, session, b=b)`, `for entry in entries`
Forbidden: do not use `node` to refer to a cost entry. `entry` is a cost-layer unit; `node` is a network neuron. Forbidden: ``entry_idx`` / ``entry_idxs`` / ``grouped_entry_idxs`` / ``_active_entry_idxs`` — the integer is the entry; use bare ``entry`` / ``entries`` / ``grouped_entries`` / ``_active_entries``. Forbidden: `record` / `records` as naming tokens for this concept — use ``entry`` / ``entries``.

### `f` / `fs`

Definition: Frequency in hertz (Hz) — oscillation / spectral peak / band limits (e.g. trace FFT analysis). Compound as `<role>_f` (`peak_f`, `min_osc_f`); collection / FFT axis `fs`.
Example: `peak_f`, `min_osc_f`, `TRACE_OSC_MIN_F`, `mask = (fs >= min_osc_f) & (fs <= max_osc_f)`, CLI `--min-f`
Forbidden: `freq`, `freqs`, `freq_hz`, `frequency`, `hz` as naming tokens for this concept.

### `figure` / `figures`

Definition: Noun qualifier for the visualization path when a name must stay distinct from the corresponding train value (kwargs, gts, filter token, CLI registration, displayed ``n_t``, ordered cells, …). Also the ``5_figure`` package and saved figure files.
Example: `figure_kwargs`, `figure_gts`, `filter_figure_token`, `add_figure_arguments`, `resolve_figure_kwargs`, `do_figure`, `figure_cells`, `figure_traces`, `n_t_figure`, `for_figure`, `figure_file_ext`, `log_figure_elapsed`, `ElapsedTimer`
Forbidden: using ``plot`` as that noun qualifier (`plot_kwargs`, `plot_gts`, `filter_plot_token`, `add_plot_*`, `do_plot`, …). Do not use ``figure`` for the verb of drawing — that is ``plot``.

### `degree` / `degrees`

Definition: An angular or visual-field quantity measured in degrees (°) — used for bar ``w``, lane pitch, hex vertex positions, and motion-axis view bounds.
Example: `x_deg, y_deg = build_hex.xy_deg_from_uv(u, v)`
Forbidden: do not use `extent` to refer to a degree quantity. `degree` is angular; `radius` is a hex-lattice distance.

### `gt` / `gts`

Definition: Ground truth target signal(s) used as the train/evaluation reference (e.g. spot/moving-bar response waveforms), and the cell set that participates in the train cost via those targets.
- ``gt`` (singular): one target waveform — one entry’s `(T,)` trace, one cell’s one-radius curve, a spot cell’s `(RF_N_RADII, n_t)` array, or a plot series that draws a single GT line.
- ``gts`` (plural): a stack/bag of those waveforms — Pack/SpotGt/MovingBarGt field ``gts`` shaped `(n_cost, T')`, or any `(n, T)` / `(n_cell, …)` tensor that holds multiple gt rows.
- Cost cell set: ``gt_cells`` / ``GT_CELLS`` / ``active_gt_cells`` — cell types optimized against gt (not a separate ``fit`` concept).
Example: `gts = torch.cat([pack.gts, extra_gts], dim=0)`, `pack.gts`, `_gather_cost_time(..., gts)`, `load_gt(...)`, `spot_gts(...)`, `gt_cells`, `GT_CELLS`
Forbidden: `fit` / `fits` / `fit_cell` / `fit_cells` / `fit_gts` / `z_fit` / `fit_parts` / `mirror` / `mirrors` / `pack_mirrors` / `mirror_sign` / `mirror_cell` / `mirror_cells` / “fit cells” / “fitted parameters” — use ``gt_cells`` / ``gts`` / ``z_best`` / ``final_parts`` / ``parameters``; update verb is ``optimize``, process noun is ``train``. Naming a multi-row gt tensor ``gt`` (use ``gts``). Naming a single waveform ``gts``. Do not use ``all_gt``. Do not suffix spot GT with ``_rt`` (`gt_rt`, `spot_gt_rts`) — radius×time shape is implied; use ``gt`` / ``gts``. Do not name pre-GT components ``rf_gt`` / ``ir_gt``. Module paths (`task.spot.gt`), CLI ``--gt``, and params ``a_gt`` / ``bias_gt`` are compounds — not this singular/plural rule.

### `group` / `groups`

Definition: A biological grouping in cell or DSI semantics — e.g. a cell-type family band in figure layout (`cell_rows`), or a PD/ND entry list pair in one DSI comparison unit. Not a cost-layer sum term.
Example: `pos_groups`, `neg_groups` in `assemble_moving_bar_dsi_groups`; `cell_rows`, `CELL_ROWS`
Forbidden: do not use `group` for cost entries summed into sub-costs — use `part`. Do not use `group` for sti b tuples forwarded together — use `b_set`.

### `h` / `hs`

Definition: Extent along the vertical axis — figure panel size (``PANEL_H`` / ``panel_h``), subplot band size (``order_hs``, ``group_h``), and other vertical layout measures. Singular ``h``; plural bag ``hs``. Compounds: ``PANEL_H``, ``order_hs``, ``group_h``.
Example: `figsize=(PANEL_W * n_col, PANEL_H * n_row)`, `order_hs`, `group_h = int(order_hs[gi])`
Forbidden: `height` / `heights` / ``HEIGHT`` / any project identifier containing ``height`` (``order_hs``, ``PANEL_HEIGHT``, ``object_height_deg``, …). Library APIs may remain (``height_ratios=``, ``get_figheight()``, plotly layout key ``"height"``).

### `hex` / `hexes`

Definition: A single hexagonal spatial position in the connectome, identified by its axial coordinates `(u, v)`.
Example: `for hex in moving_bar_cost_hexes(connectome, cost_radius=cost_radius): ...`, `shell_hexes(shell)`, `radius_hexes(radius)`
Forbidden: do not use `column` to refer to a hex position. `hex` is a spatial `(u, v)` location; `column` is a connectome position id (`column_id` / bag `column_ids`). Forbidden: ``member`` / ``members`` as naming tokens for hex bags or hex builders (`members_at_shell`, `members_in_radius`, `members_by_radius`, …) — use ``hex`` / ``hexes`` / ``shell_hexes`` / ``radius_hexes``. Forbidden: ``hexes_by_radius`` (thin ``shell_hexes`` bag wrapper) — call ``shell_hexes`` directly.

### `id` / `ids`

Definition: An external identifier from persisted or connectome source data (e.g. FAFB node `id` in `network.json`, `column_id`) — not an in-memory array position. Cell-keyed bags of those ids use ``ids_by_cell``.
Example: `node_ids`, `node_from_id`, `column_id` (disk/JSON), `connectome.column_ids` (in-memory bag), `ids_by_cell(nodes, at_u=..., at_v=...)`, `resolve_xy_ids`, `pre_id` / `post_id` (edge endpoints)
Forbidden: do not use `idx` to refer to an external/source identifier. `id` is a key stored in connectome or sidecar data; `idx` is a position in an in-memory ordered axis. Forbidden: ``instance`` / ``instances`` as naming tokens (`instance_ids`, `resolve_xy_instance_ids`, ``instance_syn_*``, prose “CELL instances”) — use ``id`` / ``ids`` / ``ids_by_cell`` / ``node``. Forbidden: ``root`` as a naming token in project identifiers (`root_id`, `root_ids`, `pre_root_id`, `post_root_id`, `column_root_ids`, ``rid``) — use ``id`` / ``ids`` / ``pre_id`` / ``post_id``; FAFB download CSV field strings ``"root_id"`` / ``"pre_root_id"`` / ``"post_root_id"`` may remain only at the load boundary.

### `idx` / `idxs`

Definition: A position in an ordered in-memory axis used for `[...]` indexing (part, packed ``z``, hex lookup, etc.). **Never a bare identifier** — always attached after the axis / object noun: ``<noun>_idx`` (singular) or ``<noun>_idxs`` (plural bag or lookup dict keyed by that noun). Bare axis integers that are the noun itself — ``t`` / ``n_t``, ``b`` / ``bs``, ``entry`` / ``entries``, ``node`` / ``nodes`` — do not take ``_idx``.
