### `key` / `keys`

Definition: A sub-option under a named parent — never the parent itself. Identifiers must use ``<parent>_key`` / ``<parent>_keys``; bare ``key`` / ``keys`` as project naming tokens are forbidden.
- ``param_key``: which attribute under a ``param`` a ``--param`` token sets — ``lo`` | ``hi`` | ``jit`` | ``init`` | ``val`` | ``mode`` (not the param name ``a_h``). ``lo``/``hi`` are the edges of a param ``clamp`` (see ``### clamp``); ``lo``/``hi``/``jit``/``init`` may take ``.NODES`` (bags ``los``/``his``/``jits``/``inits``); ``shared`` nodes must share one ``lo``, one ``hi``, and one ``jit``.
- ``i_sti_key`` / ``I_STI_KEYS``: bright / baseline / dark slots of ``i_sti``.
- ``sti_timing_key`` / ``STI_TIMING_KEYS``: ``ms_*`` / ``delta_ms*`` slots of sti timing opts.
Example: `a_h.init.L1=0.5` → param ``a_h``, param_key ``init``; `parse_param_tokens` yields ``(param, param_key, nodes, right)``; `I_STI_KEYS`; `sti_timing_key`
Forbidden: bare ``key`` / ``keys`` in project identifiers. Calling a ``param`` (``a_h``) a ``key`` / ``param_key``. ``field`` (for ``--param``); CSV ``field``. Do not call the param token ``NAME`` / ``name`` in project identifiers. Do not use ``tag`` / ``_tag`` / ``*_tag`` as a CLI / opts / parse-grammar atom — that is ``token``; e.g. ``parse_<x>_tag`` → ``parse_<x>``, filename stem ``<x>_tag`` → ``<x>_token``.

### `kwarg` / `kwargs`

Definition: Named keyword-argument bag forwarded into a callee (``dict`` or ``**`` unpack) — not CLI / session ``opt`` / ``opts``. Singular ``kwarg`` is the concept; plural ``kwargs`` is the bag, catch-all parameter, or collection of such bags. Compounds always use plural ``<role>_kwargs`` (``figure_kwargs``, ``timing_kwargs``). Catch-all signature form is always ``**kwargs`` (never ``**kw``).
Example: `resolve_figure_kwargs(args)`, `figure_kwargs`, `resolve_train_kwargs`, `resolve_run_kwargs`, `**kwargs`, `run_train_and_plot(**kwargs)`
Forbidden: `kw` as a naming token (`figure_kw`, `timing_kw`, bare `kw`, `_take_figure_kw`, `**kw`, …). Do not use `opt` / `opts` for a forwarding bag. Do not use `args` for this bag (`args` is the argparse namespace). Library API spellings that literally require a key named `subplot_kw` / `gridspec_kw` may remain only at that library call-site key; project locals / params still use `*_kwargs`.

### `label` / `labels`

Definition: Human-facing display name of a figure / report series, overlay, snapshot stage, axis, formula fragment, or console row — the string shown to the reader and, when used as a bag key, the same string. Singular ``label`` is one such name; plural ``labels`` is the ordered bag.
Example: `overlay[label]`, `overlay_labels`, `labels = ("start", "mid", "exit")`, `_BLACK_TRACE_LABELS`, `baseline_label`, formula row ``for label, series in formula``, `axis_label` returning a display ``label``.
Forbidden: ``text`` / ``*_text`` as project identifiers (library ``ax.text`` / ``get_text`` / ``set_text`` / ``write_text`` / plotly kwargs ``text=`` may remain). Do not use ``label`` as the raw CLI / opts parse input — that is ``token``. Do not use ``label`` for connectome types (``cell``), CSV columns (``field``), or param-axis CLI ids. When a formula tuple pairs a drawn string with a color-source curve name, the drawn string is ``label`` and the color source is ``series`` (not a second ``label``). Do not use ``tag`` / ``_tag`` / ``*_tag`` / ``*tag*`` for any human-facing display string, figure/report series name, overlay key, snapshot stage, axis label, or display dedup-suffix — that is ``label`` (e.g. ``hex_scope_tag`` → ``hex_scope_label``, ``_network_spot_tag`` → ``_network_spot_label``, ``row_tag`` → ``row_label``, ``panel_tag`` → ``panel_label``, ``radius_tag`` → ``radius_label``, ``*_display_tag`` → ``*_display_label``).

### `list` / `lists`

Definition: Comma-separated CLI multi-value — one argv token whose items are split on ``,``, and the ordered token bag after that split. Sole splitter is ``parse_comma_list``. CLI alias expanders for such a value use ``expand_<cli_name>_list``. CLI flags / opts / defaults that hold that comma-token (or its just-parsed tokens/values) may use the ``*_list`` compound (e.g. ``--a-h-list``, ``args.pulse_list``, ``DEFAULT_A_H_LIST``).
Example: `parse_comma_list("L1,L2")`, `--lrs 0.1,0.01`, `a_h_list = [float(x) for x in parse_comma_list(args.a_h_list)]`, `expand_ih_group_list(...)`
Forbidden: as a domain collection-bag suffix outside CLI lists (`cell_list`, `tau_list`, `cell_rows_list`, `argv_list`, `subsystem_list`, `votes_list`, `ms_pulse_list`, …) — use the plural noun alone (`cells`, `taus`, `cell_rows`, `argv`, `subsystems`, `mss`). Do not use `list` as an enumerate/print verb (`--list-parts`, `_list_parts`) — use ``--print-parts`` / ``_print_parts``. Library ``list`` / ``list[...]`` / ``list(...)`` / ``.tolist()`` / ``from_list`` may remain.

### `slice` / `slices`

Definition: Contiguous subarray along the trace time axis.
Example: `trace_slice = v_post_d[t_lo:t_hi + 1]`, `_slice_ms(v_full, start_ms, stop_ms, …)`
Forbidden: ``segment`` / ``seg`` (for trace subarrays — and banned project-wide for schema packing). Do not use `slice` for figure hex / ``--x``/``--y`` secondary curves — that is `overlay`. Do not use `slice` for pack entry or ``z`` subsetting — use `subset` (or index without coining `slice`).

### `view` / `views`

Definition: Sti coverage extent on the hex lattice, measured in degrees ``(x0, y0, x1, y1)``.
Example: `view_bounds(hexes)`, `view_bounds_from_vertices(...)`, `view_deg`, pack info ``"view_deg"``
Forbidden: ``field`` (for sti geometry); ``extent``; bare ``field_deg`` / ``field_bounds``. The geometric box uses ``bound`` / ``bounds`` (see ``### bound``); do not name it ``clamp``.

### `s` / `ss`

Definition: A time duration or absolute clock offset measured in seconds — wall-clock / SI-second timing and the seconds unit in rate compounds; never a sample index along the trace axis (that is ``t`` / ``n_t``). Parallel millisecond unit token is ``ms`` / ``mss`` (see ``### ms``).
- ``s`` (singular): one second value, or the seconds unit token in compounds (``prep_s``, ``delta_s``, ``speed_deg_over_s``).
- ``ss`` (plural): a bag/tuple/set of those values.
Example: `TraceReadout.prep_s`, `readout_prep_s(...)`, `delta_s = delta_ms / 1000.0`, `speed_deg_over_s`, `slope_mv_over_s`
Forbidden: `second` / `seconds` as naming tokens (spell the unit ``s``, as ``ms`` spells milliseconds); do not use ``s`` for a time-sample index — use ``t``; do not use ``s`` for milliseconds — use ``ms``; do not use bare ``s`` as an opaque loop/local for a non-seconds quantity (`for s in specs` → `for spec in specs`); do not glue a rate as ``<qty>_s`` without ``_over_`` (`speed_deg_s` → `speed_deg_over_s` — see ``### _over_``); do not use ``_s`` as a string-coercion suffix (`root_s`, `enabled_s`, `r_s`) or for non-time geometry (`entry_s` on a motion axis).

### `scale` / `scales`

Definition: A non-``a_*`` multiplicative coefficient — how much a quantity counts or is stretched outside network ``a_*`` params. Primary uses: cost contribution (`cost_scales`, `part_cost_scales`, `dsi_scales`, `spot_cost_radius_scale`, `_part_scale`), RF coefficients (`RF_surrnd_scale`), and other non-edge multipliers. Compounds: `<role>_scale` / plural bag `scales` / `part_cost_scales`.
Example: `pack.cost_scales`, `session.part_cost_scales`, `--part-cost-scale`, `SPOT_COST_RADIUS_SCALE`, `_scaled_cost_from_parts`, `backward_part_sums`
Forbidden: `weight` for this role (`cost_weight`, …); `factor`; `gain`. Do not use `scale` for network params — use `a_*`. Do not use `scale` for edge synaptic strength — use `weight` / `edge_weights`. Do not use `scale` for schema z↔param encoding — use `z_mode`. Do not use `scale` for Euler \(\Delta t/\tau\) step factors (`hp_scale`, `lp_scale`, `*_alpha`, …) — write the implicit|explicit update with \(\Delta t/\tau\) directly. Library matplotlib `yscale` / `set_yscale` may remain.

### `session` / `sessions`

Definition: A run-time context object that holds assembled configuration for one train/evaluation run (schema/connectome/tasks/readouts/opts).
Example: `session = train.session_from_outdir(...)`

### `shell` / `shells`

Definition: The exact-distance hex-lattice locus — hexes (or the integer index of that locus) with ``hex_radius(u, v) == N``. Singular ``shell`` is that index ``N`` or the exact-distance set; plural ``shells`` is a bag of those. Distinct from ``radius``, which sizes an **inclusive disc** (``hex_radius <= N``) or labels area/footprint extents.
Example: `shell_hexes(shell)`, `--shell N`, `ids_by_cell(nodes, shell=N)`, loop ``for shell in range(shell_max + 1)`` inside ``radius_hexes``
Forbidden: `ring` / `rings` as naming or prose substitutes for this concept. Do not use ``radius`` for exact-distance-only membership (``hex_radius == N``) — that is ``shell``. Do not use ``shell`` for inclusive discs / footprints / cost discs / RF extents — that is ``radius``. OS / process ``shell`` is out of scope.

### `sign` / `signs`

Definition: Multiplicative ±1 (or symbol ``+`` / ``−`` / ``0``) encoding the direction of a quantity — synapse E/I (`syn_sign`), cell ON/OFF (`RF_SIGN` / `rf_sign`), bright/dark sti (`contrast_sign`), peak deflection (`v_post_d_sign`). Compounds: `<role>_sign`.
Example: `contrast_sign("dark") == -1`, `rf_sign * contrast_sign(contrast) > 0`, `syn_sign`
Forbidden: `polarity` (including `polarity_sign` and prose “polarity”). Do not use `sign` for non-±1 mode enums — that is `mode` (`spot_gt_mode`, not `spot_gt_sign`).

### `spec` / `specs`

Definition: One moving-bar sti condition — direction × contrast × ``w`` (and speed), identified by token ``left_bright_w1`` / ``right_dark_w4`` / …. Type ``MovingBarSpec``; plural ``specs`` is a list of those conditions; string id is ``spec_token`` / bag ``spec_tokens``. Orthogonal to ``task`` (``moving_bar_bright`` holds many specs). Analyze report field ``"spec"`` and CLI ``--spec`` select this condition.
Example: `MovingBarSpec(direction=..., contrast=..., w_deg=...)`, `spec.token`, `gruntman_moving_bar_specs()`, `bar_specs_from_task(...)`, `parse_moving_bar_spec(token)`, `reports_by_spec[spec][cell]`, `--spec right_bright_w1`
Forbidden: component-plot layout bags (`_ComponentLayout` / ``layout``); generic config / curve / overlay bags; connectome run or path strings; ``condition`` / ``variant`` as naming tokens for this concept. Do not use ``tag`` / ``_tag`` / ``*_tag`` for a moving-bar condition id or its bag — that is ``spec`` (e.g. ``left_bright_tag`` → ``left_bright`` or ``spec_token``, ``*_spec_tag`` → ``*_spec_token``). Module files ``sti_spec`` (timing / ``i_sti`` assembly) may keep that basename. Library ``gridspec`` / ``ModuleSpec`` / ``inspect`` may remain.

### `spot` / `spots`

Definition: The spot sti task family (and its related task variants) used to generate inputs and corresponding targets.
Example: `if task_name in SPOT_TASKS: ...`

### `sti` / `stis`

Definition: Light sti drive — photoreceptor current injected at R1–6/R7/R8 (`i_sti`), and the connectome node flag/mask for those nodes (`is_sti`, JSON `"sti"`, `sti_nodes_at_uv`, `sti_nodes`, `STI_CELLS`). Compounds cover sti hex anchors and opts (`sti_uv`, `sti_opts`, `entry_sti_us`, `cost_sti_us`, `sti_b`).
Example: `i_sti = pack.i_sti`, `connectome.is_sti`, `connectome.sti_nodes`, `STI_CELLS`, `spot_sti_bs`, `build_spot_sti_opts`
Forbidden: `stimulus`, `stim`, `stimuli`, `input`, `is_input`, `PR`, `photoreceptor` (as naming tokens for this concept), `u_in`, `v_in`, `pr_idx`, `n_input_node`, `INPUT_CELLS`, `input_nodes_at`. Do not use `sti` for total ``in`` (sti + synaptic) — use `in`.

### `sum` / `sums`

Definition: A running total of values (or a bag of such totals keyed by component/cell), and the fold of many contributions into that total or into a destination tensor / cost / gradient. Singular ``sum`` is one total or the concept; plural ``sums`` is a bag of those totals. Compounds name the destination or role (``_target_sums``, ``part_sums``, ``backward_part_sums``, ``n_syn_sum``, ``b_sums``).
Example: `forward_sums.sums`, `sums[cell] += ...`, `n_syn_sum`, `part_sums`, `_target_sums`, `backward_part_sums(z, session)`
Forbidden: `accumulate` / `accum` / `acc` / `aggregate` as naming tokens or prose substitutes (``_accumulate_target`` → ``_target_sums``; ``backward_accumulate_scaled_cost`` → ``backward_part_sums``; ``accumulate_all`` → ``partner_syn_by_label``). Do not use `total` for a sum bag — `total` is the scaled cost scalar only. Do not use `count` / `counts` for a sum or cardinality. Mean/std reductions that are not sum bags use the result noun (e.g. ``_moving_bar_trace_means``), not ``sum``.

### `sum_sq` / `sum_sqs`

Definition: A running total of squared values (or a bag of such totals), kept alongside `sum` / `sums` so std can be recovered.
Example: `sum_sqs[component_key]`, `_std_from_sum_and_sum_sq`
Forbidden: `sumsq`, `accsq`, `accum_sq` — use `sum_sq` / `sum_sqs`.

### `syn` / `syns`

Definition: A synapse in the connectome: the biological connection between two cells, characterized by its sign (`syn_sign`) and count (`n_syn`), from which edge weights are derived.
Example: `syn_sign = float(e["syn_sign"])` — from `network.json` connectome data
Forbidden: do not use `edge` to refer to a synapse or its biological properties. `syn` is a connectome biological entity; `edge` is its network instantiation.

### `t` / `n_t`

Definition: Time sample index along the simulation trace axis — a position 0..`n_t`-1 into `(n_b, n_t, N)` tensors such as `trace`, or an absolute step such as `t_onset` / `cost_t0s`. The cardinality of that axis is `n_t` (how many time samples at one scope). Use compound `n_t_<noun>` when the scope is not obvious (`n_t_aligned`, `n_t_by_b`, `n_t_cost_window`, `n_t_figure`).
Example: `for t in range(n_t): ...`, `trace[:, t, node]`, `t0 + t`, `n_t = int(pack.gts.shape[1])`, `t_from_ms(ms_pre, delta_ms=delta_ms_pre)`, `cost_ts`, `pack_cost_abs_ts`
Forbidden: `win`, `win_len`, `win_lens`, `wl`, `win_idx`, `win_t`, `n_steps` — never abbreviate a time span or trace length with `win`; use `n_t` or `n_t_<noun>`. Do not suffix the time index with `_idx` — use bare `t` in loops and indexing (`for t in ...`, `trace[..., t, ...]`), not `t_idx`, `step_idx`, `time_idx`, or `cost_time_idxs`. Full-word `window` remains only in established compound names (`cost_window_*`, `TimeWindow`, `time_window`, `window_time_traces`).

### `task` / `tasks`

Definition: A train/evaluation task type (e.g., `spot_bright`, `spot_dark`, `moving_bar_bright`, `moving_bar_dark`).
Example: `for name in session.tasks: ...`

### `token` / `tokens`

Definition: (1) CLI / opts / parse grammar atom — one raw string unit still being decoded by ``parse_*``, ``split``, or ``partition`` (argv value, comma-list piece, ``KEY=VALUE`` / ``SOURCE:BOOL`` slice, ``part_key`` substring awaiting cast, or enum string id such as ``spec.token`` / mode / contrast); or (2) a stem / suffix fragment written into a **filename** (run / figure path). Singular ``token`` is one such atom; plural ``tokens`` is the ordered bag.
Example: `parse_comma_list(token)`, `parse_bool(token)`, `parse_moving_bar_spec(token)`, `parse_param_tokens(tokens)`, `enabled_token`, `radius_token = part_key[pos + 2:]`, `spec.token`, `spec_tokens`, `filter_figure_token`, `_format_filename_token`, filename stem ``filter_token``.
Forbidden: ``text`` / ``*_text`` as project identifiers (library APIs may remain — see ``### label``). Do not use ``token`` for legend / overlay / formula / title / subtitle / console display names — that is ``label``. Do not use ``token`` for ``cell``, CSV ``field``, or arbitrary ``str`` locals that are neither parse/opts atoms nor filename fragments. Do not use ``tag`` / ``_tag`` / ``*_tag`` for any CLI / opts parse atom or filename stem fragment — that is ``token`` (e.g. ``w_tag(w_deg)`` → ``w_token(w_deg)``, ``network_run_tag`` → ``network_run_token``, ``_cell_tag`` → ``_cell_token``, ``checkpoint_iter_tag`` → ``checkpoint_iter_token``).

### `total`

Definition: The single scaled overall train cost scalar — ``Σ W·part / Σ W`` over parts with non-zero scale (``W`` from `part_cost_scales` / `_part_scale`) — and plot titles/curves that refer to that scalar only.
Example: `total = _scaled_cost_from_parts(parts, session)`, `_last_total`, `"total (scaled)"` on the cost curve
Forbidden: do not use for cardinalities (`n_*`), sum bags (`sums`), plot scope/all traces, durations, or sidecar field labels.

### `trace` / `traces`

Definition: A time sequence of a signal (e.g., voltage readout waveform) represented as a tensor over time idxs.
Example: `trace, onset_trace = _forward_readout_and_onset_trace(...)`

### `train`

Definition: Noun only — the overall parameter-optimization process and its package (`import train`: session assembly, cost, optimization, persistence). Compounds use the noun (`train_opts`, `param_modes`, `TrainSession`).
Example: `session = train.session_from_outdir(...)`, `import train`
Forbidden: do not use `train` as a verb (e.g. `train_staged`, “to train”, imperative “Train …”) — the update loop verb is `optimize`. Do not use `training` as a naming token — use `train`. Do not use `fit` for this process / package (see ``### gt`` Forbidden).

### `val` / `vals`

Definition: The live numeric param (including post-train / plot-time) — not the cold-start. Singular ``val`` is one number (CLI ``--param …val…`` key, or a short-lived loop scalar). Plural ``vals`` is a bag/array of those numbers (e.g. full-w per-node arrays from ``node_vals_from_z``). Plot/analyze ``--param a_h.val.L1=0.8`` patches the current decoded vals then re-packs ``z``; it does not rewrite train ``init``/``inits``.
Example: `node_vals_from_z(z, schema)`, `z_from_node_vals(...)`, `--param a_h.val.L1=0.8`, `for key, val in opts.items(): out[key] = float(val)`
Forbidden: ``values`` as a naming token (`node_values_from_z`, …). Do not use ``val`` / ``vals`` for cold-start — that is ``init`` / ``inits``. Do not abbreviate ``val_from`` as ``vf`` / ``VF`` / ``vf_opts``.

### `val_from`

Definition: Train-opts map (CLI ``--val-from``) from target schema param to ``{source, enabled}`` — when enabled, the target parameter value is taken from another param at assignment time and the target is frozen. Bag ``VAL_FROM``; opts key ``val_from``.
Example: `resolve_val_from(args.val_from)`, `parse_val_from_tokens(...)`, `override_val_from(...)`, `val_from_enabled(opts, "bias_gt")`, `resolve_param_modes(param_modes, opts)`, `{"val_from": val_from}`
Forbidden: ``vf``, ``VF``, ``vf_opts``, or any other abbreviation. **Verb exception:** only ``val_from`` compounds may use a lexicon verb prefix (``resolve_val_from``, ``parse_val_from_tokens``, ``override_val_from``, ``add_val_from_argument``, …); this exception does not extend to other ``_from_`` identifiers.

### `w` / `ws`

Definition: Extent along the horizontal / along-track / column axis — bar angular size (``w_deg``), figure panel size (``PANEL_W`` / ``panel_w``), print-field character count, histogram bar size, pulse span in samples, and prose for full-w node arrays. Singular ``w``; plural bag ``ws``. Compounds: ``w_deg``, ``ws_deg``, ``w_token``, ``w_cols``, ``PANEL_W``, ``GRUNTMAN_WS_DEG``, ``RF_CENTER_W``.
Example: `w = float(spec.w_deg)`, `w_token(w_deg)`, `for w in GRUNTMAN_WS_DEG`, `PANEL_W`, `ws` for a bag of print-field extents
Forbidden: `width` / `widths` / ``WIDTH`` / any project identifier containing ``width`` (``w_deg``, ``ws_deg``, ``w_token``, ``full-w``, ``float w``, ``GRUNTMAN_WS_DEG``, …). Do not use bare ``w`` for cost ``scale`` or for the trainable vector ``z``. Library APIs may remain (``ax.bar(..., width=)``, ``get_figwidth()``, ``linewidth``, plotly layout key ``"width"``).

### `weight` / `weights`

Definition: Signed synaptic strength on a network `edge` — `edge_weights` from `syn_sign * n_syn` (`per_cell`) or `syn_sign` (`per_edge`), then split into excitatory/inhibitory drives (further multiplied by `a_syn_exc` / `a_syn_inh`).
Example: `edge_weights`, `ScatterConn(..., edge_weights=edge_weights, a_syn_exc=..., a_syn_inh=...)`
Forbidden: do not use `weight` for cost / DSI / RF multipliers — use `scale`. Do not use `weight` for network schema gains — use `a_*`. Do not use `factor` or `gain` as substitutes. Column-assignment vote tally is `vote_mode` (not `weight_by_syn` / `vote_by_syn`). Library `fontweight` may remain.

### `z`

Definition: The packed trainable parameter vector that optimize updates — schema packs `param` values into ``z`` and decodes them back (``param_from_z`` / ``z_from_param``; per-param encoding via ``z_mode`` ∈ {linear, log, inv}). One packed scalar (vector element or decode input) is still ``z``. Compounds: ``z_best`` (post-optimize), ``z_mode``, ``z_clamps`` (optimize numeric range bag — see ``### clamp``), ``z_init`` (cold-start vector), ``z_idx`` (position along one param's pack into ``z``), ``param_n_z``, ``schema_n_z`` (``z`` width). Builder from schema ``init``/``inits`` (+ ``jit``): ``z_init_from_schema``.
Example: `z_best, adam = optimize_staged(z, ...)`, `params_from_z(z, session)`, `param_from_z(spec, z, node=…)`, `z_from_param(spec, val)`, `z_init_from_schema(schema)`, `param.get("z_mode", "linear")`, `z_slice[z_idx]`
Forbidden: ``theta``, ``x`` as names for this vector; bare ``w`` as a name for this vector (``w`` is extent — see ``### w``); ``physical`` for decoded values (use ``param``); ``z_fit`` / ``fit`` for the returned vector (use ``z_best``; see ``### gt`` Forbidden); ``z_map`` for the encoding key (use ``z_mode``); ``guess`` / ``schema_guess`` / prose “initial guess” for the cold-start vector or its builder (use ``z_init`` / ``z_init_from_schema``); ``z_bounds`` (use ``z_clamps`` — see ``### clamp``). Do not use bare ``z`` for hex coords, trace time, or other axes. **Forbidden pack-position junk:** ``z_slot``, ``z_slot_idx`` — scalar is ``z``; pack position is ``z_idx``. **Forbidden type/container naming token ``tensor`` / ``tensors`` (project-wide):** never in identifiers (``*_tensor``, ``*_tensors``, ``pack_*_tensors``, ``_p_tensors``, ``z_tensor``, …) — name the real quantity (``params``, ``dsi``, ``entries``, ``component``, …). Library ``torch.Tensor`` / ``torch.tensor`` / ``as_tensor`` / ``is_tensor`` / ``.new_tensor`` may remain. **Also forbidden on the train vector:** ``z_t``, ``z_np``, ``z_arr``, ``z_cpu``, ``z_cuda``, and any other ``z_<dtype|device|container>`` coinage — the live vector is always ``z`` (role-qualified only as ``z_best`` / ``z_init``). Trace-oscillation ``z_threshold`` is a different concept (not this vector).

## Adjectives (A-Z)

### `active`

Definition: Modifier marking membership in the subset picked from a candidate pool — the item participates in the current scope. Realized as a list/set/tuple, predicate, or index list, not as a dense per-element 0/1 boolean array. Compound as `<noun>_active`, `active_<noun>`, or `_active_<noun>_…` in function names.
Example: `active_gt_cells(...)`, `spot_gt_active(...)`, `_pack_active_bs(...)`, `active_stis_for_subtype(...)`, `all_active`
Forbidden: `present` for this role. Do not use `active` where the value is a dense boolean 0/1 array along an axis — use `mask` in `<axis>_mask` compounds instead. Do not combine `active` and `mask` in one identifier (`*_active_*_mask`).

### `fused`

Definition: Modifier marking cost packs joined for one shared ``forward`` (verb) by concatenating `i_sti` along `b` when shape / onset match. Compound as `fused_<noun>` (`fused_packs`, `FusedPacks`).
Example: `FusedPacks`, `_build_fused_packs`, `fused_packs`
Forbidden: verb `fuse` as a naming token (`fuse_forward`, `FuseForward`, `*_fuse`, `*_fuse_*`); using verb `forward` as a noun in compounds (`FusedForward`, `fused_forward`); `fuse_id` / `*_fuse_id`; `batch` (that is `b`); do not use `group` / `b_set` for this cross-pack join.

## Verbs (A-Z)

### `add`

Definition: Register a new argument or option onto a CLI parser object.
Example: `add_train_arguments(parser)`, `add_figure_filter_argument(parser)`
Forbidden: do not use `add` for tensor arithmetic or dict insertion — `add` is exclusively for parser argument registration.

### `override`

Definition: Verb only — merge new values into an existing object (mutate in place or return the updated copy). The payload being merged is named by what it is (``inits``, ``modes``, ``param_inits``, sti opts, …), never by this verb.
Example: `override_params(...)`, `override_val_from(...)`, `override_session_sti_timing(...)`
Forbidden: `apply` (including ``apply_*``) for this merge action; using ``override`` as a noun (``init_override``, ``pack_overrides``, ``param_overrides``, bare ``overrides``, ``*_override`` bags); `edit` / `edits` / ``*_edits`` for the same merge/payload role; ``maybe`` / ``maybe_override_*`` as a conditional prefix on ``override`` (early-return when unset is implementation detail — name the verb ``override`` only).

### `build`

Definition: Create/construct a new object/structure in memory from inputs (not necessarily persistent).
Example: `spot = build_spot(connectome, spot_radius=..., multi_spot=..., fully_inside=...)`
Forbidden: `make` — use `build` instead.

### `expand`

Definition: Expand shorthand/alias inputs into a concrete list/dictionary of explicit targets.
Example: `tl = expand_tasks(list(tasks))`, `expand_gt(...)`, `expand_part_cost_scale(...)`
Forbidden: suffixing expanders with ``_dict`` (`expand_gt_dict`, `expand_part_cost_scale_dict`, …) — ``expand`` already yields the concrete bag. Comma-list CLI shape uses ``expand_<cli_name>_list`` only (see ``### list``). Do not use ``expand`` for enum validation or non-alias coerce (`standardize` / `parse`).

### `inject`

Definition: Arithmetically add a parameter-scaled contribution into an existing tensor in-place (returning the modified tensor).
Example: `i_sti = inject_a_sti_radius(i_sti, p, pack)`
Forbidden: `apply` / `override` — use `inject` only for arithmetic tensor injection; use `override` for merge into an existing object.

### `forward`

Definition: Run the full time-loop simulation on `i_sti` to produce a `(B, T, N)` readout tensor (`v` or `ca`).
Example: `v = forward_v(session, p, i_sti, pack=pack)`
Forbidden: `simulate`, `compute` — use `forward` instead.

### `load`

Definition: Load a saved artifact or serialized object from storage and reconstruct it for use.
Example: `z = train.load_best_param(outdir, session)`
Forbidden: `read` — use `load` instead.

### `normalize`

Definition: Rescale a numeric array or trace to a unit-peak (or equivalent amplitude) convention — typically baseline-subtract then divide by max absolute value.
Example: `normalize_ir(x)`, `build_ir_lti(...)` applies ``normalize_ir`` after the bandpass/LP filter
Forbidden: `rescale` as a naming token for this action — use `normalize`. Do not use `normalize` for canonical-form coercion of tokens / shapes / enums — that is `standardize`. Do not use `normalize_<noun>` to merge `const_default` bags with CLI tokens — that assembly is `resolve`.

### `optimize`

Definition: Run a staged-lr procedure that updates ``z`` to minimize `cost`.
Example: `z_best, adam = optimize_staged(z, cost_fn, z_clamps, lrs, n_iter, ...)`
Forbidden: `fit`, `train` (as a verb) — use `optimize` for the update loop. Do not name the returned ``z`` or post-optimize cost parts with `fit` (`z_fit`, `fit_parts`) — use `z_best`, `final_parts` (see ``### gt`` Forbidden).

### `parse`

Definition: Decode a raw string or token sequence into a structured in-memory value.
Example: `parse_moving_bar_spec(token)` → `(direction, contrast, w_token)`

### `plot`

Definition: Produce a visualization output (figures/curves/images) from run results or computed traces. Verb only.
Example: `plot_cost(result.cost_curve, out_path, costs_by_part=...)`, `plot_figures(...)`, `run_train_and_plot(...)`, CLI `--plot-right-only` / `plot_right_only`
Forbidden: `draw`, `render`, `visualize`, and project names built on `scatter` (e.g. “Δv scatters”) — use `plot` instead. Library `ax.scatter` / `go.Scatter` may remain. Do not use `plot` as a noun qualifier on identifiers (`plot_kwargs`, `plot_gts`, `plot_cells`, `filter_plot_token`, `add_plot_*`, `resolve_plot_kwargs`, `do_plot`, `cell_plot_rows`, …) — when the name must mark the figure path as distinct from train, use ``figure`` (see ``### figure``); otherwise drop the qualifier.

### `resolve`

Definition: Resolve abstract references/aliases/defaults into final concrete values used by execution. Use when inputs may be incomplete (CLI tokens, alias names, branch dicts, omitted keys) and the function consults ``const_default``, alias tables, and/or CLI tokens before returning.
Example: `resolve_val_from(args.val_from)`, `resolve_param_modes(param_modes, opts)`, `resolve_run_kwargs(args)`, `resolve_train_sti_timing(filter, args.sti_timing)`, `resolve_train_kwargs(args)`, `resolve_sti_timing(opts)`
Forbidden: `lookup`, `fetch`. Unnecessary `resolve` — coding-rules HARD STOP 2 (membership-only, identity, parse/expand/standardize/`_from_`, rename-to-resolve). True merge must not be named ``parse_*`` / ``build_*`` / ``merge_*`` / ``default_*`` / ``*_from_*``. ``parse_*`` decodes strings; ``expand_*`` expands aliases; defaults+CLI merge is ``resolve`` (see ``### standardize``).

### `run`

Definition: Execute a procedure end-to-end (train, evaluation, or full pipeline) and return or persist its result.
Example: `fname, outdir, session = run_train_and_plot(figure_gts=..., **kwargs)`
Forbidden: `execute`, `invoke` — use `run` instead.

### `save`

Definition: Persist in-memory arrays/objects to disk in a machine-recoverable form (e.g., `.npy/.npz` or serialized state).
Example: `np.savez_compressed(path, hex_current=hex_current)`
Forbidden: `write`, `dump`, `export` — use `save` instead.

### `standardize`

Definition: Convert an input value into a canonical/valid form (e.g. clamp rules, ordering, tensor shape, or standard representation).
Example: `standardize_cost_radius(cost_radius)`, `standardize_i_sti(session, params, i_sti, pack)` → `(B, T, N)`, `standardize_sti_timing(so)`, `standardize_option_dashes(argv)`
Forbidden: `canonicalize`, `prepare` — use `standardize` instead. Do not name the model sti hook `prep` (see `prep` for plot timing only). Do not use `standardize_<noun>` to merge `const_default` bags with CLI tokens — that assembly is `resolve` (`resolve_param_modes`, `resolve_train_sti_timing`). Do not use `normalize` for this coercion — `normalize` is amplitude rescale only.

### `step`

Definition: Advance the neuron by one time sample (`t-1 → t`) using the model dynamics. Pass model symbols inline — ``v_slow`` (hp_lp) or ``u`` / ``u_rev`` (borst) — never an opaque ``state`` bag.
Example: `v_slow, v = drv.step(v_slow, v, p, i_sti[:, t-1], session, delta_ms=...)`, `u, u_rev, v = drv.step(u, u_rev, v, p, i_sti[:, t-1], session, delta_ms=...)`
Forbidden: do not use `step` to mean an optimizer iteration — use `iter` instead. Do not name the carried internals ``state`` / ``state_tensor`` — inline ``v_slow`` or ``u`` / ``u_rev``.

## Prepositions (A-Z)

### `at_uv` / `at_xy`

Definition: Spatial locus filter — select hexes / nodes / ids at a connectome position. Always spelled with the coordinate frame: ``at_uv`` (axial ``u``/``v``) or ``at_xy`` (hex-step ``x``/``y``, not degree ``x_deg``/``y_deg``). Axis scalars are ``at_u``/``at_v`` or ``at_x``/``at_y``; bags ``at_xs``/``at_ys``. Network axial lookup uses UV (``sti_nodes_at_uv``; ``ids_by_cell(..., at_u=, at_v=)``). Figure / analyze hex filters and CLI ``--x``/``--y`` use XY (``filter_sti_hexes(..., at_x=, at_y=)``, ``align_at_x``/``align_at_y``). Connectome analyze allows either frame, mutually exclusive.
Example: `sti_nodes_at_uv(u, v)`, `filter_sti_hexes(..., at_x=, at_y=)`, `ids_by_cell(nodes, at_u=..., at_v=...)`, `ids_by_cell(nodes, at_x=..., at_y=...)`, `align_at_x`/`align_at_y`, `cli_xy_filter`, `--x`/`--y`, `--u`/`--v`
Forbidden: bare ``at`` / ``_at_`` without ``uv`` or ``xy`` (never ``ids_at_hex``, ``sti_nodes_at``, ``_nodes_at_hex``, ``hex_at_scope_label``, ``hex_at_scope_token``). Do not use ``at`` for time / node / radius-disc sampling (``bar_lane_rects_at_t``, ``_spec_at``, ``v_at_onset``, ``_component_at_nodes_*``, ``n_node_t``) — name those with the real axis noun, not ``at`` (``bar_lane_rects``, ``_node_param_key``, ``v_onset``, ``_component_nodes_*``, ``n_node_t``). Exact-distance hex selection is ``shell`` (``shell_hexes``, ``--shell``, ``ids_by_cell(..., shell=)`` — see ``### shell``), not an ``at_*`` frame. Do not use ``in``/``on``/``inside`` as naming glue for spatial membership (``inside_mask``, ``hex_in_cost_radius``, ``*_in_disc``, ``*_on_shell``, ``*_on_hex``, ``*_on_uv_line``, ``*_on_xy_line``) — use ``radius_mask`` / ``cost_radius_mask`` / ``nodes_from_hexes`` / ``hex_mask``, or ``ids_by_cell(...)`` with ``radius``/``shell``/``at_u``/``at_v``/``at_x``/``at_y``. Figure overlay bags remain ``overlay_xs``/``overlay_ys`` (see ``### overlay``); ``at_x``/``at_y`` are filter kwargs. Library ``np.add.at`` may remain.

### `_by_`

Definition: The same quantity, partitioned or indexed by a subset axis — the bag still holds that quantity, keyed by the subset noun. Pattern: `<quantity>_by_<subset>`; both sides nouns only; the full identifier has no verb token and must not also contain ``_from_``.
Example: `nodes_by_cell`, `e_leak_by_cell`, `v_th_by_cell`, `v_readout_by_cell`, `std_by_cell`, `gt_affine_by_cell`, `n_by_radius`, `n_t_by_b`, `costs_by_part`, `u_by_column_id`
Forbidden: using ``_by_`` when result and source are **different** quantities — that is ``_from_`` (`v_th_from_z`, `std_from_traces`; noun→position tables use ``<noun>_idx``, not ``idx_from_*``). Hybrids that put both prepositions in one identifier (`e_leak_by_cell_from_z`, `v_th_by_cell_from_z`). Method / “by means of” English (`vote_by_syn`, `weight_by_syn`) — that is ``mode`` (`vote_mode`). Tautology when the quantity name already encodes the subset axis (`a_sti_radius_by_radius` — the bag is ``a_sti_radius``). Do not use ``*_by_param`` / ``by_param`` / ``vals_by_param`` — the bag keyed by param is ``node_vals`` / ``schema``, not a ``_by_param`` compound. Do not use ``_by_name`` — use the real subset noun (``*_by_cell``, …). A ``_from_`` function may return a ``_by_`` bag; name the function with ``_from_`` and the bag with ``_by_`` (`v_th_by_cell = v_th_from_z(...)`).

### `_from_`

Definition: One quantity from another already-concrete quantity — result and source are different roles or types (unit/coordinate conversion, schema pack/unpack, idx lookup, ``z`` → param values, fixed formula on present inputs). Pattern: `<result>_from_<source>`; both sides nouns only; the full identifier has no verb token and must not also contain ``_by_``. This entry's Definition and Example lines use nouns only — no verb tokens.
Example: `t_from_ms(ms, delta_ms)`, `v_ca_from_v(v, p, session)`, `ca_from_v_ca(...)`, `t_abs_from_ms(...)`, `node_vals_from_z(z, schema)`, `v_th_from_z`, `e_leak_from_z`, `adams_from_z`, `hex_from_uv(u, v)`, `xy_deg_from_uv(u, v)`, `cell_from_node(...)`, `node_from_id`, `family_from_cell_csv(...)`
Forbidden: `_to_` — never use `_to_` as a directional preposition in function names or map attribute/local names. Any verb token anywhere in the identifier (`load`, `override`, `open`, `resolve`, `parse`, `build`, `assert`, …). Verbs in this lexicon entry's Definition or Example lines. Hybrids with ``_by_`` in the same identifier (see ``### _by_``). Exception: ``val_from`` compounds only (see ``### val_from``). Do not use ``_from_`` when the function merges ``const_default``, alias expansion, and/or CLI tokens into the final execution value — that is ``resolve`` (``resolve_train_kwargs``, ``resolve_sti_timing``, ``resolve_spot``), not ``kwargs_from_args``, ``timing_from_opts``, or ``spot_from_opts``. Do not use ``_from_`` for multi-source assembly (defaults + tokens + validation); one complete source → one result only. Simple field extraction from an already-final opts dict (``gt_cells_from_opts``) may remain ``_from_``; if missing keys fall back to defaults, rename to ``resolve_*``.

### `_over_`

Definition: Quotient / ratio — numerator divided by denominator. Pattern: `<numerator>_over_<denominator>`; both sides nouns or established unit tokens (``ms``, ``s``, …); the full identifier has no verb token and must not also contain ``_by_`` / ``_from_``.
Example: `dt_over_cap`, `dt_over_tau_lp`, `dt_over_tau_ca`, `dt_over_tau_hp`, `v_peak_to_peak_over_std`, `num_over_den`, `speed_deg_over_s`, `slope_mv_over_s`
Forbidden: `per` for this division role (`mv_per_s` → `mv_over_s`; `deg_per_s` / `speed_deg_s` → `speed_deg_over_s`). Established syn-weight mode tokens ``per_cell`` / ``per_edge`` are a different concept (see ``mode`` / ``weight``), not this preposition. Do not name a product with ``_over_``. Do not use ``_over_`` for partition/index (that is ``_by_``) or conversion (that is ``_from_``).
